\begin{abstract}
We present a deterministic, scale-invariant model of pulsar glitches within the Scalar-Angular-Torsion (SAT) framework. By considering pulsars as macroscopic filamental bundles interacting with a discretized 4D lattice, we reinterpret glitches as boundary effects arising when surface velocities cross a universal critical fraction of the speed of light. Applying a fractal scaling factor to match atomic-scale thresholds to neutron-star systems, we derive dimensionless surface velocity ratios and construct idealized fingerprint curves for spin jumps, glitch frequency, and luminosity modulation. This approach provides a quantitative framework for correlating glitch amplitude and rate with the surface-velocity parameter, producing falsifiable predictions for pulsar populations.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

Pulsar glitches have traditionally been modeled stochastically through superfluid vortex dynamics and crustal interactions. In the Scalar-Angular-Torsion (SAT) framework, we propose a deterministic mechanism based on geometric thresholds. The pulsar’s 3D surface velocity relative to the critical fraction of light speed governs a decoupling transition: outer layers temporarily disengage from electromagnetic interactions while retaining gravitational coupling, leading to discrete jumps in spin and observable timing anomalies.

Because the same underlying forces operate across scales, atomic-scale thresholds (e.g., technetium) can be transferred to astrophysical systems via a fractal scaling factor. This corrects prior mis-scalings that overstated the velocity threshold by orders of magnitude.

\section{Velocity-Threshold Mechanism}

Let \(R\) be the stellar radius, \(\omega\) the angular velocity, and \(v = \omega R\) the surface speed. Define the dimensionless ratio

\[
x = \frac{v}{v_{\rm crit}}
\]

where \(v_{\rm crit}\) is the scaled critical velocity for 3D decoupling. The fraction of the star’s mass participating in the decoupling transition is

\[
f_{\rm decouple}(x) = \left(1 - x^{-3}\right) H(x-1),
\]

with \(H(x)\) the Heaviside function.

The resulting change in moment of inertia produces a spin jump

\[
\frac{\Delta \omega}{\omega} \approx f_{\rm decouple}(x),
\]

while the rate of glitches is roughly proportional to the gradient

\[
f_{\rm glitch}(x) \sim \frac{d}{dx} f_{\rm decouple}(x) = \frac{3}{x^4} H(x-1).
\]

Luminosity suppression of the outer layers is similarly given by

\[
L(x) \sim 1 - f_{\rm decouple}(x).
\]

These relations generate a distinct set of curves—hereafter called the \emph{pulsar fingerprint curves}—capturing the onset, amplitude, and rate of glitches as functions of the dimensionless velocity ratio.

\section{Idealized Fingerprint Curves}

Figure~1 shows the idealized curves for spin jump, glitch rate, and luminosity fraction as functions of \(x\). To illustrate discrete glitching events, we overlay a sine-wave representation scaled by the glitch-rate proxy. Key features include:

\begin{enumerate}
\item No glitches below threshold (\(x<1\)).
\item Sharp onset of spin jumps and glitch activity at \(x \gtrsim 1\).
\item Peak glitch frequency just above threshold, decreasing at higher \(x\) as outer layers fully decouple.
\item Luminosity suppression occurring at the threshold and stabilizing at lower values as the decoupling saturates.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{figure}[h]
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{fingerprint_curve.png} % replace with actual plot in submission
\caption{Idealized SAT pulsar fingerprint curves: spin jump (\(\Delta \omega / \omega\)), glitch frequency proxy, luminosity fraction, and sine-wave representation of glitches. Dimensionless surface velocity ratio \(x=v/v_{\rm crit}\) governs the transition.}
\end{figure}

\section{Discussion and Predictions}

This framework produces several falsifiable predictions:

\begin{enumerate}
\item Glitching pulsars cluster around a narrow range of dimensionless surface velocities \(x \sim 1\).
\item The largest spin jumps occur for pulsars just exceeding the threshold, while slower pulsars rarely glitch.
\item Observed glitch frequency and amplitude should correlate with \(x\), producing a population-level “fingerprint” when plotted across pulsars.
\item Luminosity dips should coincide with glitch onset, reflecting the decoupling of surface layers from electromagnetic interactions.
\end{enumerate}

The fractal scaling factor connecting atomic-scale thresholds to astrophysical systems is essential to match observational velocities. The precise value can be tuned to fit the pulsar population, but once set, it constrains all spin-jump predictions.

\section{Conclusion}

By integrating a fractal-scaled surface velocity threshold into the SAT framework, we produce a deterministic, falsifiable model of pulsar glitches. The idealized fingerprint curves encode both the amplitude and frequency of spin jumps as functions of a single dimensionless parameter, providing a direct test against observational data. Future work will apply this model to the full known pulsar population to extract the empirical critical velocity and compare predicted versus observed glitch behavior.

